ood.\n\nWait, but `_search_regex(r'_([a-z]{2})\\.xml', sami_path, 'lang', default=...)` \u2014 for 'http://subs.example.com/captions.xml', is there a match? Pattern `_([a-z]{2})\\.xml` \u2014 'captions.xml' \u2014 hmm, 'captions.xml' \u2014 does it contain `_xx.xml`? 'captions.xml' \u2014 no underscore before 2 letters + '.xml'. Wait: 'captions.xml' \u2014 the regex needs `_` then 2 lowercase letters then `.xml`. 'captions.xml' has no underscore. OK no match \u2192 default 'lt'. \n\nHmm wait, careful: 'http://subs.example.com/captions.xml' \u2014 any `_`? No. Good.\n\nBut for the first test, sami_path='http://media.example.com/subs_fi.xml' \u2192 matches `_fi.xml` \u2192 lang='fi'. Good.\n\nNow, about `is_geo_blocked` False and formats present \u2014 the geo-restricted branch not taken. Fine.\n\nAlso, should I test the case where `streams` key is missing entirely (streams.get('streams', {}) default)? That's covered implicitly by the geo test if I pass `{}` instead of `{'streams': {}}`. Let me pass `{}` in the geo test to cover the `.get` default branch. Actually the geo test with `{'streams': {}}` vs `{}` \u2014 both give empty formats. Using `{}` covers the default-value branch of `streams.get('streams', {})`. Nice, minor branch coverage. But wait \u2014 if streams_json is `{}`, then `streams.get('streams', {})` returns `{}`. Good.\n\nHmm, but actually for the geo test, maybe include a stream that's filtered out by url_or_none to also cover line 262-263 in that test? No \u2014 line 262-263 is covered in the main test. Fine.\n\nNow \u2014 one more consideration: the `not_a_url: 12345` entry. `url_or_none(12345)`:\n\n```python\ndef url_or_none(url):\n    if not url or not isinstance(url, compat_str):\n        return None\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, actually:\n\n```python\ndef url_or_none(url):\n    if not isinstance(url, compat_str):\n        return None\n    url = url.strip()\n    return url if url else None\n```\n\nSomething like that. Either way, 12345 \u2192 None \u2192 continue. Good.\n\nAnd 'broken_rtmp': 'rtmp://host-without-app-path' \u2014 startswith 'rtmp' \u2192 regex `^(?P<url>rtmp://[^/]+/(?P<app>[^/]+))/(?P<playpath>.+)$` \u2014 'rtmp://host-without-app-path' \u2014 after `rtmp://`, `[^/]+` matches 'host-without-app-path', then needs '/' \u2014 end of string \u2192 no match \u2192 `if not m: continue`. Good, covers lines 284-285.\n\nNow, about `_match_id` \u2014 real implementation:\n\n```python\n    def _match_id(self, url):\n        if self._match_valid_url(url) is None:\n            return None\n        mobj = self._match_valid_url(url)\n        group_id = mobj.group('id') if 'id' in mobj.groupdict() else None\n        return group_id\n```\n\nHmm, in youtube_dl:\n\n```python\n    def _match_id(self, url):\n        mobj = re.match(self._VALID_URL, url)\n        assert mobj\n        return mobj.group('id')\n```\n\nSomething like that. It works with a valid URL.\n\nNow \u2014 the URL. I need to confirm the exact _VALID_URL. Let me use get_info('TVPlayIE').\n\nPotential issue: `TVPlayIE` might have `_GEO_BYPASS = False` or similar. Let me check.\n\nAnother potential issue: `TVPlayIE()` instantiation \u2014 `InfoExtractor.__init__(self, downloader=None)`:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, downloader=None):\n        self._ready = False\n        self._downloader = downloader\n        self._x_forwarded_for_ip = None\n        self._geo_bypass_country = None\n        self._geo_bypass_ip_block = None\n```\n\nSomething like that.\n\nNow about `to_screen` \u2014 used by `_initialize_geo_bypass` possibly, and `_download_json` (mocked, so no). Let me check `to_screen`:\n\n```python\n    def to_screen(self, msg, skip_eol=False):\n        \"\"\"Print message to screen if this is not a silent downloader\"\"\"\n        if self._downloader:\n            self._downloader.to_screen(\n                '%s%s' % (self.IE_NAME, ': ') + msg, skip_eol=skip_eol)\n```\n\nHmm no. Actually I recall:\n\n```python\n    def to_screen(self, msg, skip