r format dicts. Let me check what fields it needs: it uses `_formats_preference`... The real implementation:\n\n```python\ndef _sort_formats(self, formats, field_preference=None):\n    if not formats:\n        raise ExtractorError('No video formats found')\n    for f in formats:\n        ...\n    formats.sort(key=...)\n```\n\nHmm, it might require 'url' in each format? Let me check:\n\n```python\ndef _sort_formats(self, formats, field_preference=None):\n    if not formats:\n        raise ExtractorError('No video formats found')\n\n    def _formats_key(f):\n        # TODO: Sanitize when part of format is None\n        ...\n```\n\nActually in youtube_dl:\n\n```python\ndef _sort_formats(self, formats, field_preference=None):\n    if not formats:\n        raise ExtractorError('No video formats found')\n\n    def _format_key(f):\n        if field_preference:\n            return tuple(f.get(field) for field in field_preference)\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, I need to check. Let me use get_info on `_sort_formats`.\n\nActually, to be safe, I could monkeypatch `_sort_formats` too, but then line 303 executes (the call), which is what matters for coverage. But the real one running is better for correctness. Let me check its implementation first.\n\nLet me also think about the rtmp regex: `r'^(?P<url>rtmp://[^/]+/(?P<app>[^/]+))/(?P<playpath>.+)$'`. For 'rtmp://edge.example.com/app/playpath123': url='rtmp://edge.example.com/app', app='app', playpath='playpath123'. Good.\n\nFor the no-match case: 'rtmp://onlyhost' \u2014 `rtmp://[^/]+/(?P<app>[^/]+)` requires a slash after host. 'rtmp://onlyhost' has no slash after host \u2192 no match \u2192 continue. Good.\n\n**Test B: 403 HTTPError path (lines 252-255)**\n\nMock `_download_json` to raise ExtractorError with cause being a compat_HTTPError with code 403 and a `read()` method returning JSON bytes `{\"msg\": \"some message\"}`. Then `_real_extract` should raise ExtractorError with expected=True and message 'some message'.\n\nConstructing compat_HTTPError: `compat_HTTPError` is `urllib.error.HTTPError` (or `urllib2.HTTPError`). We can create one: `compat_HTTPError(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp)`. The `read` method \u2014 HTTPError.read reads from fp. We can pass a BytesIO as fp. Actually HTTPError requires fp to be a file-like object; `HTTPError.__init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp)`. If fp is None, read raises... Let's pass `io.BytesIO(b'{\"msg\": \"...\"}')`.\n\nThen the ExtractorError raised by the mocked `_download_json` must have `cause` set to this HTTPError. ExtractorError signature: `ExtractorError(msg, tb=None, expected=False, cause=None, video_id=None)`. So `ExtractorError('...', cause=http_error)`.\n\nThen in the code: `if isinstance(e.cause, compat_HTTPError) and e.cause.code == 403:` \u2192 `msg = self._parse_json(e.cause.read().decode('utf-8'), video_id)` \u2192 `raise ExtractorError(msg['msg'], expected=True)`.\n\n`_parse_json` is a real method that parses JSON. It should work with a plain string. Let me check: `_parse_json(json_string, video_id, transform_source=None, fatal=True)`. It uses `json.loads`. Should be fine.\n\nSo test B:\n\n```python\ndef test_403_error(ie, monkeypatch):\n    http_error = compat_HTTPError('http://playapi.mtgx.tv/v3/videos/stream/123', 403, 'Forbidden', {}, io.BytesIO(b'{\"msg\": \"Not available in your country\"}'))\n    def fake_download_json(url, video_id, note=None, errnote=True, fatal=True, ...):\n        if 'stream' in url:\n            raise ExtractorError('HTTP Error 403', cause=http_error)\n        return {...}\n    ...\n    with pytest.raises(ExtractorError) as exc_info:\n        ie._real_extract(url)\n    assert exc_info.value.expected\n    assert 'Not available in your country' in str(exc_info.value)\n```\n\nWait, but the first `_download_json` call is for the video JSON, and the second for streams. The mock needs to distinguish. The URLs: `http://playapi.mtgx.tv/v3/videos/%s` vs `http://playapi.mtgx.tv/v3/videos/stream/%s`. So check if '/stream/' in url.\n\n**Test C: geo restricted (lines 299-301)**\n\nStreams JSON returns `{'streams': {}}` (or streams with only invalid URLs) and video has `is_geo_blocked: True`. Then `raise_geo_restricted` is called. The real `raise_geo_restricted` raises ExtractorError. Let me check its signature in youtube_dl:\n\n```python\ndef raise_geo_restricted(self, msg, countries=None, metadata_available=False):\n    \"\"\"Raise a GeoRestrictedError\"\"\"\n    raise GeoRestrictedError(msg, ...)\n```\n\nHmm, in youtube_dl, is there a GeoRestrictedError? Let me think. youtube_dl.utils has `GeoRestrictedError`:\n\n```python\nclass GeoRestrictedError(ExtractorError):\n    \"\"\"Geographic restriction Error exception.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, msg, countries=None, **kwargs):\n        kwargs['expected'] = True\n        ...\n```\n\nAnd `InfoExtractor.raise_geo_restricted`:\n\n```python\ndef raise_geo_restricted(self, msg, countries=None, metadata_available=False):\n    if metadata_available and self._downloader.params.get('geo_bypass', True):\n        ...\n    raise GeoRestrictedError(msg, countries)\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not sure about the exact implementation. Let me check with get_info.\n\nActually wait \u2014 in the code snippet, `raise_geo_restricted` is called with just a message. The real implementation in youtube_dl (older versions):\n\n```python\ndef raise_geo_restricted(self, msg, countries=None):\n    raise ExtractorError(\n        '%s said: This video is not available in your country due to geographical restrictions. %s'\n        % (self.IE_NAME, msg), expected=True)\n```\n\nHmm. Let me just check via get_info.\n\n**Test D: sami_path subtitle with lang in path (lines 308-313)**\n\nsami_path = 'http://example.com/subs_fi.xml' \u2192 lang = 'fi'. And also test the default branch: sami_path without `_xx.xml` pattern \u2192 lang defaults to netloc TLD. E.g., sami_path = 'http://example.com/subs.xml' and url = 'http://play.tv3.lt/...' \u2192 lang = 'lt'.\n\nWait, the default: `compat_urlparse.urlparse(url).netloc.rsplit('.', 1)[-1]` \u2014 for 'play.tv3.lt' \u2192 'lt'. Good.\n\n**Test E: geo_country None (line 241-242 branch not taken)**\n\nUse a URL without 2-letter TLD... but the _VALID_URL requires specific domains which all end in 2-letter TLDs (lv, lt, ee, pl). Hmm, all the domains end in .lv, .lt, .ee, .pl \u2014 all 2-letter. So geo_country will always be found for valid URLs... unless we use the `mtg:` scheme! The _VALID_URL starts with `(?:mtg:|https?://...)`. With `mtg:1234567`, there's no http URL, so `_search_regex` for `https?://[^/]+\\.([a-z]{2})` fails \u2192 default None \u2192 geo_country is None \u2192 line 241-242 not executed (the if branch is skipped). That covers the branch where geo_country is falsy.\n\nWait, but does `mtg:` scheme exist in the _VALID_URL? Let me check. In youtube_dl's tvplay.py:\n\n```python\n_VALID_URL = r'''(?x)\n                    (?:\n                        mtg:|\n                        https?://\n                            (?:www\\.)?\n                            (?:\n                                tvplay\\.lv|\n                                tvplay\\.skaties\\.lv|\n                                latvijasradio\\.lv|\n                                tvplay\\.mtv\\.lv|\n                                delphi\\.lt|\n                                tv3play\\.lt|\n                                play\\.tv3\\.lt|\n                                tv3play\\.tv3\\.lt|\n                                sokis\\.tv3\\.lt|\n                                tv3play\\.ee|\n                                play\\.tv3\\.ee|\n                                tv3play\\.tv3\\.ee|\n                                tv3play\\.pl\n                            )\n                            /(?:[^/]+/)*\n                        )\n                        (?P<id>\\d+)\n                    '''\n```\n\nI believe `mtg:` prefix is supported. Let me verify with get_info.\n\nSo Test E: url = 'mtg:1234567', geo_country is N