        "reasoning_content": "Very useful. Key findings:\n\n1. `TVPlayIE._VALID_URL`:\n```\n(?x)\n(?:\n    mtg:|\n    https?://\n        (?:www\\.)?\n        (?:\n            tvplay(?:\\.skaties)?\\.lv(?:/parraides)?|\n            (?:tv3play|play\\.tv3)\\.lt(?:/programos)?|\n            tv3play(?:\\.tv3)?\\.ee/sisu|\n            (?:tv(?:3|6|8|10)play|viafree)\\.se/program|\n            (?:(?:tv3play|viasat4play|tv6play|viafree)\\.no|(?:tv3play|viafree)\\.dk)/programmer|\n            play\\.nova(?:tv)?\\.bg/programi\n        )\n        /(?:[^/]+/)+\n    )\n    (?P<id>\\d+)\n```\n\nSo the URL must be like `http://play.tv3.lt/programos/moterys-meluoja-geriau/409229?autostart=true` or `http://www.tvplay.lv/parraides/vinas-melo-labak/418113`. Note `/(?:[^/]+/)+` requires at least one path segment ending with slash before the id. And `mtg:418113` works.\n\nFor geo country regex `https?://[^/]+\\.([a-z]{2})`:\n- 'http://www.tvplay.lv/parraides/vinas-melo-labak/418113?autostart=true' \u2192 `[^/]+` = 'www.tvplay', `\\.`, 'lv' \u2192 geo_country='lv'. \n- 'http://play.tv3.lt/programos/moterys-meluoja-geriau/409229' \u2192 'play.tv3' + '.' + 'lt' \u2192 'lt'. \n\nWait, careful with greedy matching: `[^/]+` matches up to before first '/', i.e., 'play.tv3.lt' \u2014 then needs `\\.` \u2014 backtrack to 'play.tv3' + '.lt' \u2192 captures 'lt'. Good.\n\n- 'mtg:418113' \u2192 no match \u2192 geo_country None. \n\n2. `InfoExtractor.__init__(self, downloader=None)` calls `self.set_downloader(downloader)`. Let me check `set_downloader`:\n\n```python\n    def set_downloader(self, downloader):\n        \"\"\"Set the downloader for this instance.\"\"\"\n        self._downloader = downloader\n```\n\nProbably. It might also do more. Let me check.\n\n3. `_initialize_geo_bypass(self, geo_bypass_context)` \u2014 takes ONE argument (no `countries` param in this version!). The call in tvplay.py is `self._initialize_geo_bypass({'countries': [geo_country.upper()]})` \u2014 one arg. Good.\n\nImplementation:\n```python\nif not self._x_forwarded_for_ip:\n    if not self._downloader.params.get('geo_bypass', True):\n        return\n    if not geo_bypass_context:\n        geo_bypass_context = {}\n    if isinstance(geo_bypass_context, (list, tuple)):\n        geo_bypass_context = {'countries': geo_bypass_context}\n    ip_block = self._downloader.params.get('geo_bypass_ip_block', None)\n    if not ip_block:\n        ip_blocks = geo_bypass_context.get('ip_blocks')\n        if self._GEO_BYPASS and ip_blocks:\n            ip_block = random.choice(ip_blocks)\n    if ip_block:\n        self._x_forwarded_for_ip = GeoUtils.random_ipv4(ip_block)\n        ...\n        return\n    country = self._downloader.params.get('geo_bypass_country', None)\n    if not country:\n        countries = geo_bypass_context.get('countries')\n        if self._GEO_BYPASS and countries:\n            country = random.choice(countries)\n    if country:\n        self._x_forwarded_for_ip = GeoUtils.random_ipv4(country)\n        ...\n```\n\nSo with FakeDownloader params={} and `self._x_forwarded_for_ip` None initially:\n- `not self._x_forwarded_for_ip` \u2192 True\n- params.get('geo_bypass', True) \u2192 True\n- geo_bypass_context = {'countries': ['LT']} \u2014 not list/tuple\n- ip_block = None \u2192 ip_blocks = context.get('ip_blocks') \u2192 None \u2192 ip_block stays None\n- country = params.get('geo_bypass_country', None) \u2192 None \u2192 countries = ['LT'] \u2192 if self._GEO_BYPASS and countries \u2192 country = random.choice(['LT']) = 'LT'\n- self._x_forwarded_for_ip = GeoUtils.random_ipv4('LT')\n\nSo it calls `GeoUtils.random_ipv4('LT')`. Does GeoUtils exist in youtube_dl.utils and does random_ipv4('LT') work? The get_info output shows `from youtube_dl.utils import GeoUtils` in common.py, so GeoUtils exists. `random_ipv4(country_code)` \u2014 it looks up the country's IP blocks and generates a random IP. Does it have data for 'LT'? GeoUtils in youtube_dl:\n\n```python\nclass GeoUtils(object):\n    _COUNTRY_IP_BLOCKS = {...}\n    @staticmethod\n    def random_ipv4(country_code):\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, does youtube_dl's GeoUtils have a country IP database? In yt-dlp, GeoUtils has `_COUNTRY_IP_BLOCKS` with data. In youtube_dl... The get_info shows `from youtube_dl.utils import GeoUtils` \u2014 so it exists. Whether 'LT' is in its database \u2014 probably yes if it has a full database. But if 'LT' is not in the database, `random_ipv4` might raise or return None.\n\nRisk assessment: If `GeoUtils.random_ipv4('LT')` raises (e.g., country not found), the test fails. Let me check GeoUtils via get_info to see if 'LT' is supported.\n\nAlternatively, to be safe, I could monkeypatch `_initialize_geo_bypass` with a spy. But then line 242 still executes (the call). The postcondition assertion would be about the spy call args. That's safer. But using the real method is a better test...\n\nHmm, let me check GeoUtils.random_ipv4 first. If it supports arbitrary country codes with a big database, use real. Actually \u2014 even better: I can use a country that's definitely in the database... but the geo_country comes from the URL TLD, so I need a URL whose TLD is a supported country. Options: .lv (Latvia), .lt (Lithuania), .ee (Estonia), .se (Sweden), .no (Norway), .dk (Denmark), .bg (Bulgaria). All these are likely in GeoUtils' database if it has one.\n\nLet me check GeoUtils.\n\n4. `raise_geo_restricted` is a static method: `raise GeoRestrictedError(msg, countries=countries)`. GeoRestrictedError \u2014 get_info failed for it, but it's imported in common.py from youtube_dl.utils. Its implementation (from youtube_dl/utils.py):\n\n```python\nclass GeoRestrictedError(ExtractorError):\n    \"\"\"Geographic restriction Error exception.\n\n    This exception may be thrown when a video is not available from your\n    geographic location due to geographic restrictions imposed by a website.\n    It is expected to be thrown during extraction of a video's info.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, msg, countries=None, **kwargs):\n        countries = countries or []\n        new_msg = (\n            '%s. This video is available in %s only' % (msg, ', '.join(countries))\n            if countries else\n            '%s. This video is not available in your country due to geographical restrictions' % msg)\n        kwargs['expected'] = True\n        super(GeoRestrictedError, self).__init__(new_msg, **kwargs)\n```\n\nI'm fairly confident about this. So the raised error is GeoRestrictedError (subclass of ExtractorError), expected=True, message contains 'copyright reasons' and 'geographical restrictions'.\n\nSince `raise_geo_restricted` is a static method that just raises, using the real one is safe \u2014 no downloader access. \n\n5. `_sort_formats` \u2014 the real implementation:\n\n```python\n    def _sort_formats(self, formats, field_preference=None):\n        if not formats:\n            raise ExtractorError('No video formats found')\n        for f in formats:\n            if 'tbr' not in f and f.get('abr') is not None and (f.get('vbr') is not None):\n                f['tbr'] = f['abr'] + f['vbr']\n\n        def _formats_key(f):\n            from ..utils import determine_ext\n            if not f.get('ext') and 'url' in f:\n                f['ext'] = determine_ext(f['url'])\n            if isinstance(field_preference, (list, tuple)):\n                return tuple(...)\n            preference = f.get('preference')\n            if preference is None:\n                preference = 0\n                if f.get('ext') in ['f4f', 'f4m']:\n                    preference -= 0.5\n            protocol = f.get('protocol') or determine_protocol(f)\n            proto_preference = 0 if protocol in ['http', 'https'] else -0.5 if protocol == 'rtsp' else -0.1\n            if f.get('vcodec') == 'none':\n                preference -= 50\n                if self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats'):\n                    ...\n```\n\nNote: `self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats')` is accessed inside `_formats_key` \u2014 but only in the `if f.get('vcodec') == 'none':` branch or the else branch? Looking at the code: the `self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats')` appears in both branches (vcodec == 'none' and else). Wait:\n\n```python\n            if f.get('vcodec') == 'none':\n                preference -= 50\n                if self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats'):\n                    ORDER = ['aac', 'mp3', 'm4a', 'webm', 'ogg', 'opus']\n                else:\n                    ORDER = ['webm', 'opus', 'ogg', 'mp3', 'aac', 'm4a']\n                ext_preference = 0\n                ...\n            else:\n                if f.get('acodec') == 'none':\n                    preference -= 40\n                if self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats'):\n                    ORDER = ['flv', 'mp4', 'webm']\n                else:\n                    ORDER = ['webm', 'flv', 'mp4']\n                ...\n```\n\nSo `self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats')` IS accessed for every format (in the else branch when vcodec != 'none'). So `_downloader` must not be None and must have `params`. FakeDownloader with params={} handles this: `.get('prefer_free_formats')` \u2192 None \u2192 falsy \u2192 ORDER = ['webm', 'flv', 'mp4'].\n\nAlso `determine_protocol(f)`:\n\n```python\ndef determine_protocol(f):\n    protocol = f.get('protocol')\n    if not protocol:\n        url = f.get('url')\n        if url:\n            protocol = determine_ext(url).lower()  # no...\n```\n\nActually:\n\n```python\ndef determine_protocol(f):\n    \"\"\" Determine protocol (http, https, rtsp, rtmp, ...) from URL \"\"\"\n    protocol = f.get('protocol')\n    if not protocol:\n        url = f.get('url')\n        if url:\n            protocol = compat_urllib_parse_urlparse(url).scheme\n            ...\n    return protocol or 'http'\n```\n\nSomething like that. For our formats with 'url', it parses the scheme. For rtmp format, url='rtmp://edge.example.com/liveapp' \u2192 protocol 'rtmp' \u2192 proto_preference = -0.1 (not http/https/rtsp). Fine.\n\nSo real `_sort_formats` works with FakeDownloader. \n\nBut wait \u2014 one more check: `determine_protocol` might use `f.get('protocol')` and if url scheme is 'rtmp'... it's fine.\n\nSo I can use the real `_sort_formats`. \n\n6. `to_screen(self, msg)`: `self._downloader.to_screen('[%s] %s' % (self.IE_NAME, msg))` \u2014 requires `self._downloader` to exist and have to_screen. FakeDownloader has to_screen. Good. But note: `to_screen` is called by `_initialize_geo_bypass` only when `params.get('verbose', False)` \u2014 with params={}, verbose is False, so to_screen is NOT called. Good, but FakeDownloader has it anyway.\n\nWait, actually in `_initialize_geo_bypass`, `self._downloader.to_screen('[debug] ...')` is called directly (not self.to_screen) only if verbose. With params={}, skipped.\n\n7. `ExtractorError.__init__`: note `if video_id is not None: msg = video_id + ': ' + msg` and `if cause: msg += ' (caused by %r)' % cause`. And `self.msg`? The get_info output doesn't show `self.msg = msg`! It shows:\n\n```python\n        super(ExtractorError, self).__init__(msg)\n        self.traceback = tb\n        self.exc_info = sys.exc_info()\n        self.cause = cause\n        self.video_id = video_id\n```\n\nHmm, no `self.msg`! And `expected`? It's not stored either?? Wait, the output shows:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, msg, tb=None, expected=False, cause=None, video_id=None):\n        if sys.exc_info()[0] in (compat_urllib_error.URLError, socket.timeout, UnavailableVideoError):\n            expected = True\n        if video_id is not None:\n            msg = video_id + ': ' + msg\n        if cause:\n            msg += ' (caused by %r)' % cause\n        if not expected:\n            msg += bug_reports_message()\n        super(ExtractorError, self).__init__(msg)\n        self.traceback = tb\n        self.exc_info = sys.exc_info()\n        self.cause = cause\n        self.video_id = video_id\n```\n\nHmm, `expected` is not stored as an attribute in this version?! Interesting \u2014 in this youtube_dl version, `expected` only affects the message (bug_reports_message appended or not). So I can't assert `excinfo.value.expected`. Instead, I should assert on the message: for expected errors, the message does NOT contain the bug report message (\"please report this issue on https://yt-dl.org/bug ...\" or similar). And `str(excinfo.value)` == the msg (since `super().__init__(msg)` sets args).\n\nWait, but `bug_reports_message()`:\n\n```python\ndef bug_reports_message():\n    if ytdl_is_updateable():  # no...\n    ...\n```\n\nActually:\n\n```python\ndef bug_reports_message():\n    if ytdl_is_updateable():\n        update_command = 'youtube-dl -U'\n    else:\n        update_command = 'pip -U youtube-dl'  # hmm\n    msg = ('; please report this issue on https://yt-dl.org/bug .'\n           ' Make sure you are using the latest version; see'\n           ' https://yt-dl.org/update on how to update.'\n           ' Be sure to call youtube-dl with the --verbose flag and include its complete output.')\n    return msg\n```\n\nSomething like that. So for the 403 test: `raise ExtractorError(msg['msg'], expected=True)` \u2192 message is just msg['msg'] (no bug report suffix). For the re-raise test (line 256): the original ExtractorError is re-raised \u2014 its message already includes whatever was set at construction.\n\nSo assertions:\n- 403 test: `str(excinfo.value)` contains 'not available in your country' and does NOT contain 'please report this issue' (expected=True means no bug report message). Hmm, wait \u2014 careful: `ExtractorError(msg['msg'], expected=True)` \u2014 msg['msg'] = 'This content is not available in your country'. expected=True \u2192 no bug_reports_message. So str = 'This content is not available in your country'. \n\nHmm wait, but there's a subtlety: `if sys.exc_info()[0] in (compat_