ting compat_HTTPError: `compat_HTTPError` is `urllib.error.HTTPError` (or `urllib2.HTTPError`). We can create one: `compat_HTTPError(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp)`. The `read` method \u2014 HTTPError.read reads from fp. We can pass a BytesIO as fp. Actually HTTPError requires fp to be a file-like object; `HTTPError.__init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp)`. If fp is None, read raises... Let's pass `io.BytesIO(b'{\"msg\": \"...\"}')`.\n\nThen the ExtractorError raised by the mocked `_download_json` must have `cause` set to this HTTPError. ExtractorError signature: `ExtractorError(msg, tb=None, expected=False, cause=None, video_id=None)`. So `ExtractorError('...', cause=http_error)`.\n\nThen in the code: `if isinstance(e.cause, compat_HTTPError) and e.cause.code == 403:` \u2192 `msg = self._parse_json(e.cause.read().decode('utf-8'), video_id)` \u2192 `raise ExtractorError(msg['msg'], expected=True)`.\n\n`_parse_json` is a real method that parses JSON. It should work with a plain string. Let me check: `_parse_json(json_string, video_id, transform_source=None, fatal=True)`. It uses `json.loads`. Should be fine.\n\nSo test B:\n\n```python\ndef test_403_error(ie, monkeypatch):\n    http_error = compat_HTTPError('http://playapi.mtgx.tv/v3/videos/stream/123', 403, 'Forbidden', {}, io.BytesIO(b'{\"msg\": \"Not available in your country\"}'))\n    def fake_download_json(url, video_id, note=None, errnote=True, fatal=True, ...):\n        if 'stream' in url:\n            raise ExtractorError('HTTP Error 403', cause=http_error)\n        return {...}\n    ...\n    with pytest.raises(ExtractorError) as exc_info:\n        ie._real_extract(url)\n    assert exc_info.value.expected\n    assert 'Not available in your country' in str(exc_info.value)\n```\n\nWait, but the first `_download_json` call is for the video JSON, and the second for streams. The mock needs to distinguish. The URLs: `http://playapi.mtgx.tv/v3/videos/%s` vs `http://playapi.mtgx.tv/v3/videos/stream/%s`. So check if '/str