r exception.\n\n    This exception may be thrown when a video is not available from your\n    geographic location due to geographic restrictions imposed by a website.\n    It is expected to be thrown during extraction of a video's info.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, msg, countries=None, **kwargs):\n        countries = countries or []\n        new_msg = (\n            '%s. This video is available in %s only' % (msg, ', '.join(countries))\n            if countries else\n            '%s. This video is not available in your country due to geographical restrictions' % msg)\n        kwargs['expected'] = True\n        super(GeoRestrictedError, self).__init__(new_msg, **kwargs)\n```\n\nAnd in common.py:\n\n```python\n    def raise_geo_restricted(self, msg, countries=None, metadata_available=False):\n        \"\"\"Raise a GeoRestrictedError\"\"\"\n        if metadata_available and self._downloader.params.get('geo_bypass', True):\n            ...\n        raise GeoRestrictedError(msg, countries)\n```\n\nHmm, the `metadata_available` param was added later. In older youtube_dl:\n\n```python\n    def raise_geo_restricted(self, msg, countries=None):\n        raise GeoRestrictedError(msg, countries)\n```\n\nThe call in tvplay.py is `self.raise_geo_restricted('This content might not be available in your country due to copyright reasons')` \u2014 one arg. So either signature works.\n\nIf GeoRestrictedError is raised, `pytest.raises(ExtractorError)` catches it (subclass). And `expected` is True. The message would be 'This content might not be available in your country due to copyright reasons. This video is not available in your country due to geographical restrictions'. I can assert `'copyright reasons' in str(excinfo.value)`.\n\nBut wait \u2014 does `raise_geo_restricted` use `self._downloader`? If the newer version checks `self._downloader.params.get('geo_bypass', True)` when metadata_available... but metadata_available defaults to False, so probably not accessed. Let me c